E-2 / T2.9 -- default word width
================================

Gates BinMat's default template argument, which every kernel inherits. Compares
binCV against binCV at four word widths, so ARCHITECTURE 10.3's OpenCV
denominator does not apply.

DECISION RULE, WRITTEN BEFORE MEASURING (TASKS.md T2.9, recorded in
EXPERIMENTS.md X-10 at commit 4245210, and in benchmark/wordwidth_benchmark.cpp):
  * uint64_t wins by > 10% on bulk kernels AND does not increase footprint at
    representative widths -> change the default
  * within 10%, OR footprint increases at small pyramid levels -> keep uint32_t
    (memory wins ties)
And the trap T2.9 names: "measure footprint at 94x60, not only at 640x480, or
this experiment will reach the wrong conclusion."

PLATFORM: the footprint half is exact integer arithmetic and closes anywhere; the
speed half is AUTHORITATIVE only here. This is the most 32-bit-sensitive of the
three experiments -- on armv7l every uint64_t operation is synthesised from
32-bit pairs -- and the runner refuses to run on anything but aarch64.

  device:           pi4
  cpu:              Raspberry Pi 4 Model B Rev 1.5 (Cortex-A72)
  arch / kernel:    aarch64 / 6.18.34+rpt-rpi-v8
  compiler:         g++ (Debian 14.2.0-19) 14.2.0, Release (-O2 -DNDEBUG)
  governor:         performance (restored to ondemand on exit)
  core pinning:     taskset -c 3
  throttled before / after: throttled=0x0 / throttled=0x0  (every run)
  benchmark commit: 0072c1a

RE-MEASURED AT COMMIT 0072c1a, superseding the first three runs (commit 3383996).
The harness's physical-bound sanity check derived the cache tier from TRAFFIC
(three images per bitwiseAnd call) and printed it as the working set, so it
reported "112.5 KiB, inside its 1 MiB L2" for a batch holding 337.5 KiB inside a
1.32 MiB interleaved round; and the shared timing loop restarted its input
rotation every batch. Both were fixed and committed before this re-run. Neither
moved a ratio -- every number below reproduces the original conclusion.

