READING -- AND THE TWO HALVES OF THE RULE POINT OPPOSITE WAYS, EXACTLY AS X-10's
HYPOTHESIS SAID THEY WOULD.

Speed: uint64_t is a LARGE win on the reduction -- 1.94x at 640x480 and 1.56x at
94x60, reproducible to 0.3% -- and a null result on bitwiseAnd (0.945-0.970x
against a 7-12% batch spread; that kernel is memory-bound, and all four widths
move identical bytes at 640x480). Narrow words are much worse on the reduction:
uint8_t runs at 0.25x, which is the per-word cost of the popcount lowering paid
eight times as often. So the ">10% on bulk kernels" clause is satisfied outright.

Footprint: uint64_t costs +33.3% at 94x60 and +20.0% at 160x120, and nothing at
either full frame. The penalty appears exactly where T2.9 warned it would -- the
upper pyramid levels LK touches every frame -- and had this been measured only at
640x480 every row would have read 0.0% and the experiment would have concluded
the opposite.

The rule is a CONJUNCTION, and its second clause fires: footprint increases at
small pyramid levels, so uint32_t stays the default. Memory wins ties, and this
is not even a tie -- it is a documented tiebreak resolving a real disagreement.
The 1.94x is declined, not overlooked.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE. The width is a per-object template parameter (D-1), so
nothing stops a pyramid from using uint64_t at the levels where it costs no bytes
(L0, L1) and uint32_t above them. That is a real lever this experiment has now
priced on both sides, but it is a NEW decision -- per-level word width -- that no
experiment has registered, and it is not taken here.

X-7 CAVEAT, at its sharpest on this axis. On x86_64 __builtin_popcountll lowers
to a library call PER WORD whatever the word is, which flatters narrow words and
can invert this ranking outright; on aarch64 it is fmov/cnt/uaddlv/fmov. The
countNonZero column above is therefore a measurement of the target's lowering as
much as of the width, and only this device can rank it. No -march flag was added:
that is a dispatch decision (ROADMAP 2.3) no experiment has settled.

ABSOLUTE ns/pixel HERE IS NOT COMPARABLE WITH X-6's LOGIC NUMBERS. This fixture
holds nine images per width (four input pairs plus a destination) and interleaves
four widths, so a batch streams from L2; logic_benchmark holds one triple and
streams largely from L1. The RATIOS are unaffected -- all four widths sit in the
same fixture -- but bitwiseAnd uint32 reads ~0.033 ns/px here against ~0.023
there. See results/logic_benchmark_pi4.log, which reproduces both.

